metabolism in the brain. In this study, we sought to
compare the effect of the oral intake of soy peptide with
casein peptide at a lower dose on l-Tyr and catecholamine neurotransmitter metabolism in the mouse brain.
MATERIALS AND METHODS
Materials. A soy peptide mixture, Hinute-AM, was
provided by Fuji Oil Co. Ltd. (Osaka, Japan). A casein
peptide mixture, Peptopro, was obtained from DSM Food
Specialities (Delf, The Netherlands). These commercially
available peptide products are composed primarily of diand tripeptides. Adrenaline (also known as epinephrine),
dopamine (DA), 3,4-dihydroxyphenylacetic acid (DOPAC),
5-hydroxyindoleacetic acid (5-HIAA), 5-hydroxytrypt­
amine (5-HT), homovanillic acid (HVA), 3-methoxytyramine (3-MT), NA, normetanephrine (NM), and
3-methoxy-4-hydroxyphenylethyleneglycol (MHPG) were
obtained from Sigma-Aldrich (St. Louis, MO).
Animal experiments. All animal experiments in this
study followed the “Standards Relating to the Care and
Management of Laboratory Animals and Relief of Pain”
(Notice no. 88, Ministry of the Environment, Government of Japan). All experiments and procedures were
reviewed and approved by the Animal Experiment Committee of Kyushu University (Permit Number: A25-121
and A27-103). Male C57BL/6N mice at 8 wk of age were
purchased from Charles River Laboratories Japan, Inc.
(Kanagawa, Japan). They were maintained at the Biotron
Institute of Kyushu University on a 12 h light/12 h dark
cycle (lights on 8 a.m. to 8 p.m.) in an air-conditioned
room (2561˚C) with 60% humidity under specific
pathogen-free conditions. They were then transferred to
the breeding room at the laboratory in a controlled environment described above and kept overnight with access
to water and standard laboratory chow. After one night
of fasting, mice were divided into three groups, and a
peptide solution (0.026 g/0.6 mL/30 g body weight) in
saline or saline alone was administered to each group
by oral gavage. Mice were sacrificed 30 min after oral
administration, and the brains and blood were sampled. The cerebral cortex, hippocampus, and brainstem
were individually isolated on an ice-cooled plastic plate,
weighed, frozen in liquid N2, and stored at 280˚C. Sera
were prepared by centrifuging blood at 20,000 3g for
10 min after overnight standing still at 4˚C. Serum samples were stored at 220˚C.
Amino acid analysis. Amino acids in the serum and
left cerebral hemisphere were determined using ultra
high-performance liquid chromatography (UPLC).
Briefly, after homogenization in 5 volumes of sterilized
water, each brain sample was centrifuged at 20,400 3g
for 30 min at 4˚C. Sera were diluted 3-fold in sterilized
water. Proteins in the supernatant and diluted sera were
removed by adding a 1/10 volume of 60% perchloric
acid, and the solution was adjusted to pH 7–8 with 8 m
KOH. Then the amino acid composition of the supernatant was determined with the Acquity UPLC H-Class
(Waters Ltd., Milford, MA) with Empower software
(Waters Ltd.) and a system consisting of a reversedphased column (YMC-Triart C18, S-1.9 mm, 12 nm,

10032.0 mm I.D.; YMC Co., Ltd., Kyoto, Japan). The
gradient elution buffer consisted of 100% methanol
and 50 mm sodium acetate buffer (0.25 mm acetic acid
contained) and the flow rate was 0.25 mL/min at 25˚C.
Determination of monoamines and their metabolites.
Catecholamines, serotonin, and their metabolites in the
right cerebral hemisphere, hippocampus and brainstem
were determined by an HPLC-electrochemical detector
(ECD) system HTEC-500 (Eicom Co., Ltd., Kyoto, Japan)
according to the manufacturer’s instructions. In brief,
frozen brain samples were homogenized in 5 volumes
of ice-cold 0.2 m perchloric acid containing 0.1 mm
